    )
    select
      'Practice 6. A Survivor’s Story',
      'User-provided Reading practice. Full answer key and explanations included.',
      30, 'DRAFT', actor_id, 'READING', 'SINGLE_SKILL', 'v1.0',
      '["PRACTICE", "READING", "PASSAGE_1", "ANSWERED", "EXPLANATIONS_INCLUDED"]'::jsonb,
      $${"format":"PASSAGE_1","sectionsPreset":"PASSAGE_1","expectedQuestions":13,"sourceUrl":"https://realieltsexams.com/san-pham/a-survivors-story/?view=exam&questionnaire_id=437","passages":[{"id":"survivor-passage-1","passageNo":1,"title":"A Survivor’s Story","content":"<p>As an island country with a fauna dominated by birds. New Zealand was once home to an owl species which is now extinct, the laughing owl, named for its distinctive cry. This bird was widespread throughout the islands when European settlers arrived in the middle of the 19th century and it remained in good numbers for some years thereafter. Where other native birds suffered from predation by the Polynesian rat, the laughing turned the tables and adapted its diet to include the rodent. It was also capable of catching and killing the other New Zealand owl, the Morepork, and even larger birds, such as the weka. However, the laughing owl was wiped out around the beginning of the 20th century. Its demise was caused by specimen collectors, habitat changes, and non-native predators including cats and stoats. Surprisingly, it is the smaller owl, the morepork, that has managed to survive until this day.</p><p>Speckled dark brown, with yellow eyes and long tails, they are around 29 centimeters long from head to tail and 175 grams in weight. Moreporks have fringes on the edge of their feathers, so they can fly almost silently and not alert potential prey. They have acute hearing and their large eyes are very sensitive to light.</p><p>Moreporks nest in tree hollows, in clumps of plants, or in cavities among rocks and roots, in the wild, moreporks usually start nesting in October, although zoo specimens have been recorded nesting in midwinter, possibly stimulated by an ample food supply. The female lays up to three white eggs, which she incubates for 20 to 30 days. During this time, she rarely hunts, and the male brings food to her. Once the chicks hatch, she stays mainly in the nest until the young owls are fully feathered. When hatched chicks are covered in light grey down, and have their eyes closed. The eyes do not open until the eighth day after hatching. They can fly for around 35 days.</p><p>By day, moreporks sleep in roots. By night, they hunt a variety of animals – mainly large invertebrates including the scarab and huhu beetles, months, caterpillars and spiders. They also take small birds and mice. They can find suitable food in pine forests as well as native forest. A morepork uses its sharp talons to catch or stun its prey, which it then carries away in its bill. Moreporks are clever hunters, and birds such as robins, grey warblers and fantails can end up as their prey. In the day, these small birds sometimes mob drowsy moreporks and chase them away from their roosts. They force the sleepy predators to search for a more peaceful spot.</p><p>Moreporks have proved to be ungracious hosts. Scientists trying to establish a population of plovers on Motuora island in New Zealand’s Hauraki Gulf were mystified as to why only two birds survived out of 75 placed there. The culprits turned out to be five pairs of Moreporks that ate or chased away the new arrivals.</p><p>Although moreporks are still considered to be relatively common, it is likely that numbers are in gradual decline due to predation and loss of habitat. As the female is a hole-nester, she is vulnerable to predators such as stoats and possums during the breeding season, and eggs and chicks are also at risk from rats. The use of pesticides is another possible threat to the owls though not a direct one. As moreporks are at the top of the food chain, they could be affected by an accumulative poison by consuming prey that has ingested poison.</p><p>The New Zealand Department of Conservation is taking steps to ensure the preservation of New Zealand’s only native owl. The department is involved in measuring the population of moreporks and has put transmitters on a number of birds to determine survival and mortality. As well as being New Zealand’s only native owl, the morepork has symbolic and spiritual importance, so in monitoring the birds it is hoped that the morepork will continue to survive and thrive.</p><p>At dusk, the melancholy sound of the morepork can be heard in forests and parks as it calls toother moreporks and claims territory. Its Maori name (ruru) echoes its two-part cry, in the tradition of the Maori people of New Zealand, the morepork, or ruru, was often seen as a careful guardian. A number of sayings referred to the bird’s alertness. As a bird of the night, it was associated with the spirit world. Moreporks were believed to act as messengers to the gods in the heavens, flying along spiritual paths in the sky. They were the mediums used to communicate with the gods. The occasional high, piercing call of the morepork signified bad news, but the lower-pitched and more common ‘ruru’ call heralded good news.</p>","questionGroups":[{"id":"survivor-group-1","title":"Questions 1–7","titleMode":"CUSTOM","startQuestionNo":1,"endQuestionNo":7,"typeFormat":"TRUE_FALSE_NOT_GIVEN","instructions":"Choose TRUE if the statement agrees with the information given in the text, choose FALSE if the statement contradicts the information, or choose NOT GIVEN if there is no information on this.","answerSource":"PASSAGE","questions":[{"id":"survivor-q1","number":1,"typeFormat":"TRUE_FALSE_NOT_GIVEN","prompt":"Early European settlers made detailed studies of the morepork.","correctAnswers":["NOT_GIVEN"],"acceptableAnswers":[],"maxScore":1,"explanation":"NOT GIVEN. The passage says that the laughing owl was widespread when European settlers arrived, but it does not say that the settlers made detailed studies of the morepork.","evidenceQuote":"This bird was widespread throughout the islands when European settlers arrived","solutionVisibility":"STUDENT_AFTER_SUBMIT"},{"id":"survivor-q2","number":2,"typeFormat":"TRUE_FALSE_NOT_GIVEN","prompt":"The Polynesian rat had a negative effect on the number of laughing owls.","correctAnswers":["FALSE"],"acceptableAnswers":[],"maxScore":1,"explanation":"FALSE. The passage contrasts the laughing owl with other native birds: instead of suffering from the Polynesian rat, the laughing owl adapted its diet to include the rodent.","evidenceQuote":"Where other native birds suffered from predation by the Polynesian rat, the laughing turned the tables","solutionVisibility":"STUDENT_AFTER_SUBMIT"},{"id":"survivor-q3","number":3,"typeFormat":"TRUE_FALSE_NOT_GIVEN","prompt":"The laughing owl was larger than the morepork.","correctAnswers":["TRUE"],"acceptableAnswers":[],"maxScore":1,"explanation":"TRUE. The morepork is described as the smaller owl, so the laughing owl was larger than it.","evidenceQuote":"it is the smaller owl, the morepork, that has managed to survive","solutionVisibility":"STUDENT_AFTER_SUBMIT"},{"id":"survivor-q4","number":4,"typeFormat":"TRUE_FALSE_NOT_GIVEN","prompt":"Rats pose a risk to young moreporks.","correctAnswers":["TRUE"],"acceptableAnswers":[],"maxScore":1,"explanation":"TRUE. The passage explicitly states that eggs and chicks are at risk from rats.","evidenceQuote":"eggs and chicks are also at risk from rats","solutionVisibility":"STUDENT_AFTER_SUBMIT"},{"id":"survivor-q5","number":5,"typeFormat":"TRUE_FALSE_NOT_GIVEN","prompt":"The New Zealand Department of Conservation is hoping to limit the population of moreporks.","correctAnswers":["FALSE"],"acceptableAnswers":[],"maxScore":1,"explanation":"FALSE. The department is taking steps to preserve the species and hopes that moreporks will continue to survive and thrive, not reduce their population.","evidenceQuote":"it is hoped that the morepork will continue to survive and thrive","solutionVisibility":"STUDENT_AFTER_SUBMIT"},{"id":"survivor-q6","number":6,"typeFormat":"TRUE_FALSE_NOT_GIVEN","prompt":"Other bird species are frightened away when they hear the morepork’s cry.","correctAnswers":["NOT_GIVEN"],"acceptableAnswers":[],"maxScore":1,"explanation":"NOT GIVEN. The passage says that small birds sometimes mob and chase drowsy moreporks away from their roosts during the day, but it does not say they are frightened away by the morepork’s cry.","evidenceQuote":"these small birds sometimes mob drowsy moreporks and chase them away from their roosts","solutionVisibility":"STUDENT_AFTER_SUBMIT"},{"id":"survivor-q7","number":7,"typeFormat":"TRUE_FALSE_NOT_GIVEN","prompt":"In Maori tradition, the low call of the morepork had negative associations.","correctAnswers":["FALSE"],"acceptableAnswers":[],"maxScore":1,"explanation":"FALSE. The high, piercing call was associated with bad news, whereas the lower-pitched and more common ‘ruru’ call heralded good news.","evidenceQuote":"the lower-pitched and more common ‘ruru’ call heralded good news","solutionVisibility":"STUDENT_AFTER_SUBMIT"}]},{"id":"survivor-group-2","title":"Questions 8–13","titleMode":"CUSTOM","startQuestionNo":8,"endQuestionNo":13,"typeFormat":"NOTE_COMPLETION","instructions":"Write ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.","answerSource":"PASSAGE","questions":[{"id":"survivor-q8","number":8,"typeFormat":"NOTE_COMPLETION","prompt":"Approximately __________ in length.","correctAnswers":["29 centimeters"],"acceptableAnswers":["29 centimetres"],"maxScore":1,"explanation":"The answer is “29 centimeters”. The passage gives the morepork’s length as around 29 centimeters from head to tail.","evidenceQuote":"they are around 29 centimeters long from head to tail","solutionVisibility":"STUDENT_AFTER_SUBMIT"},{"id":"survivor-q9","number":9,"typeFormat":"NOTE_COMPLETION","prompt":"Nests in trees, plants or spaces in roots and __________.","correctAnswers":["rocks"],"acceptableAnswers":[],"maxScore":1,"explanation":"The answer is “rocks”. Moreporks nest in tree hollows, clumps of plants, or cavities among rocks and roots.","evidenceQuote":"in cavities among rocks and roots","solutionVisibility":"STUDENT_AFTER_SUBMIT"},{"id":"survivor-q10","number":10,"typeFormat":"NOTE_COMPLETION","prompt":"Transports its prey using its __________.","correctAnswers":["bill"],"acceptableAnswers":[],"maxScore":1,"explanation":"The answer is “bill”. The morepork uses its sharp talons to catch or stun prey and then carries it away in its bill.","evidenceQuote":"which it then carries away in its bill","solutionVisibility":"STUDENT_AFTER_SUBMIT"},{"id":"survivor-q11","number":11,"typeFormat":"NOTE_COMPLETION","prompt":"Can be chased away by other birds during the __________.","correctAnswers":["day"],"acceptableAnswers":[],"maxScore":1,"explanation":"The answer is “day”. Small birds sometimes mob drowsy moreporks and chase them away from their roosts in the day.","evidenceQuote":"In the day, these small birds sometimes mob drowsy moreporks","solutionVisibility":"STUDENT_AFTER_SUBMIT"},{"id":"survivor-q12","number":12,"typeFormat":"NOTE_COMPLETION","prompt":"Attacked __________ that had been introduced to Motuora Island.","correctAnswers":["plovers"],"acceptableAnswers":[],"maxScore":1,"explanation":"The answer is “plovers”. Five pairs of moreporks ate or chased away the plovers introduced to Motuora Island.","evidenceQuote":"Scientists trying to establish a population of plovers on Motuora island","solutionVisibility":"STUDENT_AFTER_SUBMIT"},{"id":"survivor-q13","number":13,"typeFormat":"NOTE_COMPLETION","prompt":"May be exposed to __________ in their prey.","correctAnswers":["poison"],"acceptableAnswers":[],"maxScore":1,"explanation":"The answer is “poison”. Moreporks may consume prey that has already ingested poison, causing the poison to accumulate in the food chain.","evidenceQuote":"they could be affected by an accumulative poison by consuming prey that has ingested poison","solutionVisibility":"STUDENT_AFTER_SUBMIT"}],"wordLimitRule":"ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER","gapFillLayout":"LIST","gapFillTemplate":"Approximately [[1]] in length.\nFeathers with fringes to enable quiet flight.\nNests in trees, plants or spaces in roots and [[2]].\nAfter about 35 days, baby moreporks are capable of leaving the nest.\nTransports its prey using its [[3]].\nCan be chased away by other birds during the [[4]].\nAttacked [[5]] that had been introduced to Motuora Island.\nMay be exposed to [[6]] in their prey."}]}]}$$::jsonb
    where not exists (select 1 from public.tests where title = 'Practice 6. A Survivor’s Story');
  end if;
